# 67. Development Workflow

---

# 1. Purpose

The Development Workflow defines the complete lifecycle of work within the Loyalty Platform project.

It standardizes how Product Decisions become production software while ensuring consistency between Product, Architecture, Engineering, QA, DevOps and AI implementation agents.

The workflow minimizes ambiguity, prevents architectural drift and provides complete traceability from idea to production.

---

# 2. Scope

This workflow applies to:

- Product changes
- Architecture changes
- Feature implementation
- Bug fixes
- Refactoring
- Infrastructure changes
- Documentation changes
- Security improvements
- Release activities

Every implementation activity follows this workflow.

---

# 3. Guiding Principles

The workflow is built on the following principles:

- Blueprint First
- Architecture Before Code
- Small Independent Tasks
- Single Module Ownership
- Continuous Verification
- Documentation Synchronization
- Human Approval for Critical Changes
- AI-Assisted Development
- Traceability by Default
- Continuous Improvement

---

# 4. Development Lifecycle

Every implementation follows the same lifecycle.

```
Business Need

↓

Product Decision

↓

Blueprint

↓

Engineering Playbook

↓

Product Backlog

↓

Task Catalog

↓

Module Implementation Package

↓

Task Assignment

↓

Implementation

↓

Review

↓

QA

↓

Merge

↓

Release

↓

Monitoring

↓

Production Feedback
```

Each stage produces deliverables that become inputs for the next stage.

---

# 5. Phase 1 – Product Definition

Owned by:

- Product Owner

Objectives:

- identify business problem;
- define business value;
- approve Product Decision;
- determine implementation priority.

Deliverables:

- Product Decision
- Business Requirements
- Acceptance Criteria

No implementation begins before Product approval.

---

# 6. Phase 2 – Blueprint

Owned by:

- Product Owner
- Solution Architect

Objectives:

- define business rules;
- define domain model;
- define events;
- define APIs;
- define permissions;
- define architecture.

Deliverables:

- Blueprint documentation

Blueprint becomes the authoritative product specification.

---

# 7. Phase 3 – Engineering Preparation

Owned by:

- Solution Architect

Objectives:

- identify owning module;
- prepare implementation strategy;
- update Engineering documentation;
- verify dependencies.

Deliverables:

- Engineering references
- Module ownership
- Definition of Done
- UAT references

---

# 8. Phase 4 – Planning

Owned by:

- Product Owner
- Project Manager

Objectives:

- create backlog item;
- create implementation task;
- assign priority;
- identify dependencies.

Deliverables:

- Product Backlog item
- Project Task

Only Ready tasks may proceed.

---

# 9. Phase 5 – Task Preparation

Owned by:

- Solution Architect

Objectives:

- assign Knowledge Package;
- identify Blueprint references;
- identify Engineering references;
- define allowed files;
- define forbidden files;
- identify mandatory reviewers.

Deliverables:

- Complete implementation context

No implementation starts without a completed Module Implementation Package.

---

# 10. Phase 6 – Task Assignment

Owned by:

- Project Manager
- AI Orchestration System

Objectives:

- assign implementation agent;
- reserve task ownership;
- verify dependencies;
- verify readiness.

Every task has exactly one implementation owner.

---

# 11. Phase 7 – Implementation

Owned by:

- Assigned Implementation Agent

Objectives:

- implement scope;
- remain inside module boundaries;
- follow Coding Standards;
- execute mandatory tests;
- update documentation if required.

Implementation agents must not:

- expand scope;
- modify unrelated modules;
- invent Product Decisions;
- violate ownership rules.

---

# 12. Phase 8 – Self Verification

Before requesting review, the implementation agent must verify:

- compilation;
- linting;
- formatting;
- unit tests;
- integration tests;
- documentation updates;
- Definition of Done.

Incomplete work must not enter review.

---

# 13. Phase 9 – Technical Review

Owned by:

- Review Agents
- Human Reviewers

Review verifies:

- Blueprint compliance;
- Engineering compliance;
- Coding Standards;
- architecture consistency;
- maintainability;
- security;
- documentation.

Possible outcomes:

- Approved
- Approved with Comments
- Changes Requested
- Rejected

---

# 14. Phase 10 – QA Validation

Owned by:

- QA Agent
- QA Engineer

Validation includes:

- mandatory tests;
- regression tests;
- UAT preparation;
- API verification;
- security validation;
- concurrency validation.

QA does not review implementation quality.

QA verifies behavior.

---

# 15. Phase 11 – Merge

Owned by:

- Repository Maintainer

Requirements:

- review approved;
- QA approved;
- documentation updated;
- CI passing;
- Definition of Done satisfied.

Merge must preserve repository stability.

---

# 16. Phase 12 – Release

Owned by:

- Release Manager

Activities:

- deployment;
- smoke testing;
- monitoring;
- rollback verification;
- release documentation.

Release follows:

**60-release-strategy.md**

---

# 17. Phase 13 – Production Monitoring

Owned by:

- DevOps
- Platform Operations

Activities:

- monitor logs;
- monitor metrics;
- monitor business KPIs;
- verify integrations;
- detect incidents.

Monitoring begins immediately after deployment.

---

# 18. Phase 14 – Feedback Loop

Feedback sources include:

- customer reports;
- support tickets;
- analytics;
- monitoring;
- AI recommendations;
- business stakeholders.

Feedback may produce:

- bug reports;
- backlog items;
- Product Decisions;
- ADRs.

The workflow is iterative.

---

# 19. AI Agent Workflow

AI implementation follows this sequence:

```
Receive Task

↓

Load Knowledge Package

↓

Read Blueprint

↓

Read Engineering Documents

↓

Verify Scope

↓

Implement

↓

Execute Tests

↓

Generate Evidence

↓

Submit for Review
```

AI agents must never skip Knowledge Package loading.

---

# 20. Human Collaboration

Humans remain responsible for:

- Product Decisions;
- architecture approval;
- production releases;
- security approval;
- business acceptance.

AI assists implementation but does not replace governance.

---

# 21. Required Deliverables

Every completed implementation task must produce:

- implementation summary;
- changed files;
- executed tests;
- review evidence;
- readiness level;
- documentation updates;
- rollback confirmation.

Deliverables become permanent project artifacts.

---

# 22. Documentation Synchronization

Documentation must remain synchronized with implementation.

If implementation changes:

- APIs;
- Events;
- Domain ownership;
- Database;
- Security;
- Configuration;

the corresponding documentation must be updated before the task is closed.

Documentation drift is considered a project defect.

---

# 23. Exception Handling

When unexpected situations occur:

- identify the issue;
- determine whether Blueprint already covers it;
- create a Product Decision if required;
- create an ADR if architecture changes;
- update backlog if implementation changes.

Implementation must never silently redefine project rules.

---

# 24. Workflow Metrics

Recommended workflow metrics include:

Planning

- Ready Tasks
- Blocked Tasks

Execution

- Lead Time
- Cycle Time
- Review Time

Quality

- Review Pass Rate
- QA Pass Rate
- Defect Escape Rate
- Rework Rate

Operations

- Deployment Frequency
- Rollback Frequency
- Production Incidents

Documentation

- Documentation Update Rate
- Documentation Drift

These metrics support continuous improvement.

---

# 25. Continuous Improvement

The workflow itself should be reviewed regularly.

Improvements may be proposed by:

- Product Owner
- Solution Architect
- Engineering Leads
- QA
- DevOps
- AI Review Agents

Workflow changes require architectural approval.

---

# 26. Anti-Patterns

The following practices are prohibited:

- coding without a Task;
- coding without a Knowledge Package;
- coding without Blueprint references;
- changing business rules during implementation;
- merging without review;
- releasing without QA;
- updating production without Release Strategy;
- leaving documentation outdated;
- bypassing Definition of Done.

These practices increase project risk and are not permitted.

---

# 27. Definition of Development Workflow Ready

The Development Workflow is considered ready when:

- the complete lifecycle is defined;
- ownership is defined for every phase;
- required deliverables are defined;
- AI workflow is defined;
- human responsibilities are defined;
- review process is defined;
- QA process is defined;
- release process is defined;
- monitoring process is defined;
- documentation synchronization rules are defined;
- workflow governance is defined.

The Development Workflow becomes the authoritative operational process for all implementation activities following Blueprint Freeze v1.0.